# Lee’s Market: FSMA 204 traceability workflow findings

Prepared for the Regulatory Affairs report lead. These are operating recommendations based on FDA materials; the company’s current system gaps have not been independently verified.

## Product scope to establish first

FSMA 204 applies to foods specifically listed on FDA’s Food Traceability List (FTL), plus foods containing an FTL food as an ingredient when that ingredient remains in its listed form. A department name alone does not decide coverage. Lee’s should map exact SKUs, species, forms, recipes, preparation steps, business activity, and any claimed exemption before configuring requirements. [FDA Food Traceability List](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/food-traceability-list) · [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule)

| Lee’s category | Practical scope check |
|---|---|
| Fresh produce | Some specified items and forms are listed, including cucumbers, herbs, leafy greens, melons, peppers, sprouts, tomatoes, tropical tree fruits, and fresh-cut produce. Do not treat all fresh produce as covered; examples and exclusions depend on the exact item and form. |
| Seafood | Listed categories include specified finfish (including fresh, frozen, or previously frozen forms and smoked finfish), crustaceans, and bivalve molluscan shellfish. Some fish and shellfish forms are excluded or subject to exemptions; identify species and product form. |
| Meat and poultry | Not standalone FTL categories generally. Continue food safety and lot controls under applicable requirements, and assess any product containing a listed ingredient separately. Meat salads are expressly excluded from the FTL ready-to-eat deli-salad category. |
| Bakery | Not a general FTL category. Assess products and ingredients individually; the ingredient’s form and any processing step can change whether the finished food is covered. Do not infer that all egg-containing baked goods are covered. |
| Prepared foods | Certain refrigerated ready-to-eat deli salads are listed (for example, egg, potato, pasta, and seafood salad; not meat salad). Other prepared foods may be covered if an FTL ingredient remains in its listed form. FDA examples distinguish fresh ingredients in bagged salad, smoothies, or sandwiches from processed forms such as spinach on frozen pizza. |

Source for all category distinctions: [FDA Food Traceability List](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/food-traceability-list) and [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule). Product-by-product coverage and retail exemptions require confirmation against current FDA guidance and counsel; a direct-to-consumer sale definition is not a blanket exemption for all retailer handling.

## Recommended end-to-end workflow

1. **Qualify suppliers and product records.** Maintain an FTL decision for each relevant SKU/ingredient, with exact description, species or produce type, form, recipe/preparation step, supplier, and exemption rationale. Require suppliers of covered foods to transmit the traceability lot code (TLC), lot-linked key data elements (KDEs), and a usable reference document. Test the data for imported goods and supplier substitutions as well as domestic supply.
2. **Capture receiving data at case/lot level.** At each covered receipt, scan or enter the product description, quantity and unit, TLC, immediate previous source location, receiving location and date, TLC source location (or permitted source reference), and reference document type/number. Match the captured lot and quantity to the invoice/ASN; quarantine data exceptions until resolved. FDA identifies receiving as a critical tracking event (CTE) and specifies KDEs for it. [FDA Rule overview](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) · [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule)
3. **Preserve lot identity through store handling.** Keep the received TLC linked to inventory, location, date, and subsequent use. When an operation qualifies as transformation, preserve the link from every input lot to the resulting output lot; record input TLCs and quantities, output product and quantity, transformation location/date, output TLC/source, and the supporting reference. Configure repacking, cutting, mixing, and prepared-food recipes for review rather than assuming they are or are not transformations. [FDA Rule overview](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) · [FDA CTE/KDE chart](https://www.fda.gov/files/food/published/FSMA%20Rule%20for%20Food%20Traceability%20-%202024-0520-CTEs-KDEs.pdf)
4. **Record inter-site and business-to-business movements.** For covered lots shipped to another company location or business recipient, capture product, amount/unit, immediate subsequent recipient location, ship-from location/date, TLC/source, and reference document; send required shipping KDEs to the recipient. FDA’s shipping definition excludes a sale or shipment directly to a consumer. For online picking, substitutions, and home delivery, retain an internal order-to-lot link voluntarily so Lee’s can identify affected customers if a lot is recalled. [FDA Rule overview](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) · [FDA recordkeeping fact sheet](https://www.fda.gov/media/169510/download)
5. **Exercise a targeted withdrawal and customer response.** Run mock recalls that start from an inbound TLC and trace to every store, transformed batch, on-hand quantity, business recipient, and (where records permit) online order. Check that teams can place a hold, reconcile affected units, retrieve FDA-requested records promptly, and prepare a factual customer notice. Use the chain to narrow product holds and provide timely, targeted notices; these are expected operational benefits, not measured outcomes in the FDA sources.

## Prioritized implementation checklist and measures

**Priority 1 — Scope and ownership:** Build the SKU/ingredient coverage register and assign owners in regulatory, buying, receiving, store operations, IT, food preparation, and e-commerce. Have counsel validate exemption decisions and U.S./Canada cross-border roles. Measure: share of candidate SKUs reviewed; share of decisions with a documented rationale and owner.

**Priority 2 — Supplier and receiving data:** Add lot/KDE and document requirements to supplier onboarding; test receipt capture in representative stores for produce, seafood, imported goods, and weight-based items. Measure: covered receipts with complete, matched records; missing/invalid TLC rate; exception resolution time; supplier completeness rate.

**Priority 3 — Store transformations and distribution:** Pilot lot-linked prep/repacking records and inter-site shipping, then expand to stores and distribution points. Measure: transformed batches with all inputs linked to output; lots traceable across site transfers; quantity reconciliation rate.

**Priority 4 — Recall, delivery, and trust:** Connect lots to inventory and online order lines where feasible, rehearse a trace and customer-notification workflow, and publish only claims Lee’s can support with records. Measure: time to identify affected stock and orders; completeness of affected-quantity reconciliation; time to hold product; time to prepare a customer notice; customer inquiry resolution and post-incident trust feedback. FDA states records must be made available to FDA within 24 hours unless another reasonable time is agreed; Lee’s can set a shorter internal mock-recall goal as an operational target, not a rule deadline. [FDA recordkeeping fact sheet](https://www.fda.gov/media/169510/download)

Likely failure modes to test (not confirmed current Lee’s gaps): inconsistent supplier product names or lot formats; lost case-to-unit lot links; language/alias or species mismatches; weight-based produce handling; repacking and commingling; prepared-food recipe changes; cross-store transfers; and online substitutions that break order-to-lot mapping.

## Evidence limits

FDA materials explain requirements and the intended public-health benefit of faster identification/removal of potentially contaminated foods. They do not quantify Lee’s Market’s current readiness, recall-time improvement, cost savings, illness reduction, or customer-trust impact. Establish internal baselines before claiming business outcomes. Retail-related exemptions and product coverage should be confirmed against the current FAQ and applicable rule provisions before implementation.
